1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,310
Imagine you're floating in the middle of the ocean.

2
00:00:03,310 --> 00:00:06,361
You look in every direction and see nothing but

3
00:00:06,361 --> 00:00:09,801
water. The nearest land is two thousand seven hundred

4
00:00:09,801 --> 00:00:13,177
kilometers away. The nearest people aren't on a ship

5
00:00:13,177 --> 00:00:16,292
or an island. They're in space, flying over your

6
00:00:16,292 --> 00:00:19,408
head. This is Point Nemo, the loneliest place on

7
00:00:19,408 --> 00:00:22,199
Earth. And by the end of this video, you'll

8
00:00:22,199 --> 00:00:24,341
see why you'd never survive here.

9
00:00:24,891 --> 00:00:28,311
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

10
00:00:28,311 --> 00:00:32,549
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

11
00:00:32,549 --> 00:00:34,854
going to the middle of nowhere.

12
00:00:35,404 --> 00:00:39,263
Point Nemo is in the South Pacific Ocean. Officially,

13
00:00:39,263 --> 00:00:43,486
it's called the oceanic pole of inaccessibility: the point

14
00:00:43,486 --> 00:00:46,689
in the ocean that is farthest from any land.

15
00:00:47,239 --> 00:00:51,224
Nobody discovered it by sailing there. In 1992, a

16
00:00:51,224 --> 00:00:55,616
survey engineer named Hrvoje Lukatela found it using a

17
00:00:55,616 --> 00:01:00,821
computer, by calculating the point farthest from every coastline

18
00:01:00,821 --> 00:01:01,959
on the planet.

19
00:01:02,509 --> 00:01:06,715
It's named after Captain Nemo, the submarine captain from

20
00:01:06,715 --> 00:01:10,994
Jules Verne's novel Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Sea.

21
00:01:10,994 --> 00:01:14,166
In Latin, nemo means no one. A perfect name

22
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:16,675
for a place where there is no one.

23
00:01:17,225 --> 00:01:21,618
There's an even creepier coincidence. In 1928, the horror

24
00:01:21,618 --> 00:01:25,858
writer H. P. Lovecraft placed his fictional sunken city

25
00:01:25,858 --> 00:01:29,403
of R'lyeh, home of the monster Cthulhu, in the

26
00:01:29,403 --> 00:01:33,180
South Pacific. The coordinates he gave are only a

27
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:37,265
few hundred kilometers from the real Point Nemo, more

28
00:01:37,265 --> 00:01:40,734
than sixty years before anyone calculated it.

29
00:01:41,284 --> 00:01:44,726
The three closest pieces of land are all about

30
00:01:44,726 --> 00:01:49,814
two thousand six hundred eighty-eight kilometers away. Ducie Island,

31
00:01:49,814 --> 00:01:53,331
part of the Pitcairn Islands to the north. Motu

32
00:01:53,331 --> 00:01:56,473
Nui, a tiny islet off Easter Island to the

33
00:01:56,473 --> 00:02:00,663
northeast. And Maher Island, off the coast of Antarctica

34
00:02:00,663 --> 00:02:01,636
to the south.

35
00:02:02,186 --> 00:02:05,104
To give you an idea, that's a bit farther

36
00:02:05,104 --> 00:02:08,377
than from Mexico City to Los Angeles. And none

37
00:02:08,377 --> 00:02:11,509
of those islands even have a permanent town.

38
00:02:12,059 --> 00:02:16,713
Here's the strangest part. The International Space Station orbits

39
00:02:16,713 --> 00:02:20,794
about four hundred kilometers above Earth. When it passes

40
00:02:20,794 --> 00:02:24,445
over the South Pacific, the astronauts on board are

41
00:02:24,445 --> 00:02:27,739
often the closest humans to Point Nemo by far.

42
00:02:28,289 --> 00:02:31,467
Think about that. If you were stranded here, the

43
00:02:31,467 --> 00:02:34,579
people nearest to you would be living in space,

44
00:02:34,579 --> 00:02:38,486
moving at almost twenty-eight thousand kilometers per hour.

45
00:02:39,036 --> 00:02:42,249
And at night, the darkness is total. There are

46
00:02:42,249 --> 00:02:45,602
no city lights for thousands of kilometers. On a

47
00:02:45,602 --> 00:02:48,816
clear night, the sky would be filled with more

48
00:02:48,816 --> 00:02:52,867
stars than most people ever see. Beautiful, and terrifying

49
00:02:52,867 --> 00:02:54,055
at the same time.

50
00:02:54,605 --> 00:02:58,035
Quick question. Could you handle being this alone, even

51
00:02:58,035 --> 00:03:00,343
for one day? Tell me in the comments.

52
00:03:00,893 --> 00:03:05,118
Because nobody lives anywhere near it, Point Nemo has

53
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:09,103
a very unusual job. It's where space agencies send

54
00:03:09,103 --> 00:03:10,856
old spacecraft to die.

55
00:03:11,406 --> 00:03:14,987
When a big satellite or space station reaches the

56
00:03:14,987 --> 00:03:18,495
end of its life, controllers fire its engines to

57
00:03:18,495 --> 00:03:22,002
slow it down. It falls back into the atmosphere,

58
00:03:22,002 --> 00:03:25,875
mostly burns up, and the surviving pieces splash down

59
00:03:25,875 --> 00:03:28,579
in the empty ocean around Point Nemo.

60
00:03:29,129 --> 00:03:32,426
The biggest one so far was the Russian space

61
00:03:32,426 --> 00:03:36,246
station Mir. In 2001, about one hundred twenty tons

62
00:03:36,246 --> 00:03:38,869
of it came down in this part of the

63
00:03:38,869 --> 00:03:43,438
ocean. Hundreds of other spacecraft, mostly cargo ships, rest

64
00:03:43,438 --> 00:03:45,087
on the sea floor here.

65
00:03:45,637 --> 00:03:49,961
And the International Space Station itself is planned to

66
00:03:49,961 --> 00:03:53,590
end up here too. Around 2030, it's scheduled to

67
00:03:53,590 --> 00:03:57,141
be steered down into this same remote patch of

68
00:03:57,141 --> 00:03:57,605
ocean.

69
00:03:58,155 --> 00:04:01,131
So let's say your ship sinks and you're left

70
00:04:01,131 --> 00:04:04,310
at Point Nemo with a life jacket. What happens?

71
00:04:04,310 --> 00:04:06,880
The first problem is the water itself.

72
00:04:07,430 --> 00:04:11,451
Point Nemo sits far south, near the latitudes sailors

73
00:04:11,451 --> 00:04:15,624
call the Roaring Forties and Furious Fifties. The water

74
00:04:15,624 --> 00:04:19,569
is cold, usually well under fifteen degrees Celsius.

75
00:04:20,119 --> 00:04:24,071
Cold water pulls heat from your body about twenty-five

76
00:04:24,071 --> 00:04:27,512
times faster than cold air. In water this cold,

77
00:04:27,512 --> 00:04:31,172
hypothermia can make you lose control of your arms

78
00:04:31,172 --> 00:04:34,246
and legs within an hour or two, and become

79
00:04:34,246 --> 00:04:36,076
deadly after a few hours.

80
00:04:36,626 --> 00:04:40,199
And the ocean here is about four kilometers deep.

81
00:04:40,199 --> 00:04:43,845
There's nothing to stand on, nothing to hold, just

82
00:04:43,845 --> 00:04:45,522
water all the way down.

83
00:04:46,072 --> 00:04:50,095
The second problem is the weather. Strong westerly winds

84
00:04:50,095 --> 00:04:54,908
blow around the Southern Ocean almost without interruption, because

85
00:04:54,908 --> 00:04:58,500
there are hardly any continents to slow them down.

86
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:02,163
They whip up huge waves, often several meters high,

87
00:05:02,163 --> 00:05:03,672
sometimes far bigger.

88
00:05:04,222 --> 00:05:08,189
Sailors in round-the-world yacht races pass not far from

89
00:05:08,189 --> 00:05:11,165
Point Nemo. Many describe it as one of the

90
00:05:11,165 --> 00:05:14,990
scariest parts of the whole trip, because if something

91
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:17,470
goes wrong, rescue could take days.

92
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:20,664
What would you want with you if you were

93
00:05:20,664 --> 00:05:23,704
stranded at Point Nemo? One item only. Tell me

94
00:05:23,704 --> 00:05:24,761
in the comments.

95
00:05:25,311 --> 00:05:28,689
The third problem is food and water. You can't

96
00:05:28,689 --> 00:05:33,022
drink seawater, it makes dehydration worse. And unlike many

97
00:05:33,022 --> 00:05:36,106
oceans, this one has very little to catch.

98
00:05:36,656 --> 00:05:40,316
Point Nemo sits inside the South Pacific Gyre, a

99
00:05:40,316 --> 00:05:44,509
giant, slowly spinning loop of ocean current. The water

100
00:05:44,509 --> 00:05:48,322
in the middle receives very few nutrients, so tiny

101
00:05:48,322 --> 00:05:52,744
plankton struggle to grow. Without plankton, there are few

102
00:05:52,744 --> 00:05:53,125
fish.

103
00:05:53,675 --> 00:05:57,800
Scientists call areas like this ocean deserts. The water

104
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:00,968
around the gyre is some of the clearest and

105
00:06:00,968 --> 00:06:05,388
emptiest on Earth. Beautiful, but terrible for anyone trying

106
00:06:05,388 --> 00:06:06,198
to survive.

107
00:06:06,748 --> 00:06:09,661
Deep on the sea floor, there is some life:

108
00:06:09,661 --> 00:06:13,685
strange crabs and microbes near volcanic vents, living off

109
00:06:13,685 --> 00:06:18,055
chemicals instead of sunlight. But that's kilometers below you.

110
00:06:18,605 --> 00:06:22,973
In 1997, underwater microphones run by the US government

111
00:06:22,973 --> 00:06:26,561
picked up an incredibly loud, low sound in the

112
00:06:26,561 --> 00:06:30,930
remote South Pacific. People nicknamed it the Bloop, and

113
00:06:30,930 --> 00:06:34,986
some guessed it was a giant sea creature. Scientists

114
00:06:34,986 --> 00:06:38,808
later concluded it was most likely an icequake, a

115
00:06:38,808 --> 00:06:41,773
huge iceberg cracking near Antarctica.

116
00:06:42,323 --> 00:06:45,877
Even here, humans have left a mark. In 2018,

117
00:06:45,877 --> 00:06:50,884
sailors in a round-the-world race collected water samples near

118
00:06:50,884 --> 00:06:54,761
Point Nemo and found tiny pieces of plastic. Not

119
00:06:54,761 --> 00:06:58,557
even the loneliest place on Earth is untouched.

120
00:06:59,107 --> 00:07:02,523
And the biggest problem of all: nobody is coming

121
00:07:02,523 --> 00:07:06,010
quickly. Point Nemo is far from the main shipping

122
00:07:06,010 --> 00:07:09,283
lanes. A rescue ship might need many days just

123
00:07:09,283 --> 00:07:10,137
to get here.

124
00:07:10,687 --> 00:07:14,235
Even most planes can't fly there and back without

125
00:07:14,235 --> 00:07:18,218
refueling. Your best hope would be an emergency beacon,

126
00:07:18,218 --> 00:07:20,969
a strong life raft, and a lot of luck.

127
00:07:21,519 --> 00:07:25,016
In water alone, without a raft, you'd likely last

128
00:07:25,016 --> 00:07:28,655
hours. With a raft, shelter and supplies, you might

129
00:07:28,655 --> 00:07:31,795
last days. But at Point Nemo, help is almost

130
00:07:31,795 --> 00:07:33,935
always farther away than that.

131
00:07:34,485 --> 00:07:38,169
People have survived long ordeals at sea, but only

132
00:07:38,169 --> 00:07:42,000
with luck and skill. In 1982, sailor Steven Callahan

133
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,610
drifted for seventy-six days in a small life raft

134
00:07:45,610 --> 00:07:49,146
across the Atlantic. He caught fish with a spear

135
00:07:49,146 --> 00:07:52,535
gun and made drinking water with a small solar

136
00:07:52,535 --> 00:07:55,924
still. And the Atlantic has far more ships and

137
00:07:55,924 --> 00:07:59,019
islands than the waters around Point Nemo.

138
00:07:59,569 --> 00:08:02,476
So that's Point Nemo. A spot no one has

139
00:08:02,476 --> 00:08:05,606
ever lived, found by a computer, used as a

140
00:08:05,606 --> 00:08:10,079
graveyard for spacecraft, where the nearest people are often

141
00:08:10,079 --> 00:08:12,091
astronauts flying overhead.

142
00:08:12,641 --> 00:08:15,631
It's a reminder of how huge our oceans are.

143
00:08:15,631 --> 00:08:19,455
They cover about seventy-one percent of the planet, and

144
00:08:19,455 --> 00:08:22,305
we've explored only a small part of them.

145
00:08:22,855 --> 00:08:25,778
The Pacific alone is bigger than all of the

146
00:08:25,778 --> 00:08:28,972
land on Earth put together. You could fit every

147
00:08:28,972 --> 00:08:32,370
continent inside it and still have room left over.

148
00:08:32,920 --> 00:08:36,798
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

149
00:08:36,798 --> 00:08:38,403
Tell me in the comments.

150
00:08:38,953 --> 00:08:42,727
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

151
00:08:42,727 --> 00:08:45,884
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

152
00:08:45,884 --> 00:08:48,767
friend who loves the ocean. See you in the

153
00:08:48,767 --> 00:08:49,385
next one.

